<p id="p8">The actual game was filmed from a variety of angles, including from the side of each team’s goal and from high above the stadium via drone. I don’t know if the editing on these sorts of immersive videos has gotten better or if I’ve just gotten more used to the changes in point of view, but I found it very easy to keep up with the action and orient myself even as the film cut between different angles. The documentary intersperses game footage with shots of cheering fans in the stadium, in a restaurant, and at home to give viewers the full experience of rooting for Real Madrid.</p>
<p id="p9">When the credits rolled, the film left me feeling like I’d been on a real journey with the team and the fans watching them give their all on the pitch. It was thrilling to see the action play out in such close proximity, and it really felt like I was in the stadium for those 20 minutes. I walked away both satisfied and eager to experience a game in this way again as soon as possible; I think that’s exactly how a film like this one should leave its viewers.</p>
<p id="p10">As with any sport, live event, or destination, Apple Immersive Video continues to be the best way to experience something besides actually being there. Immersive content is one of the best use cases for the Apple Vision Pro, and personally, I can’t get enough of it.</p>
<p id="p11"><em>Real Madrid: The Weight of Greatness</em> debuts in the TV app on Apple Vision Pro this Friday, and it’s a great watch whether you’re a Real Madrid die-hard or, like me, someone without any history with the team. I recommend checking it out.</p>
